<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tlingit</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tlingit people</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tlingit are an Indigenous people of the Pacific Northwest coast, living in south-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaska">Alaska</a> and neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-columbia">British Columbia</a>. Organised in clans and known for their carving and potlatches, they resisted Russian expansion, destroying the fort at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sitka">Sitka</a> in 1802 before being defeated in the Battle of Sitka in 1804.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tlingit.jpg" alt="Tlingit" width="320" height="346" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Case, W.H. (William Howard) (1868-1920) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chief_Anotklosh_of_the_Taku_Tribe_in_Juneau.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Tlingit, or Lingít, speak the Tlingit language, an independent branch of the Na-Dené family, and their name for themselves means People of the Tides. Their culture developed in the temperate rainforest of the south-east Alaskan coast and the Alexander Archipelago. Culture with elements related to the Tlingit originated around 10,000 years ago near the mouths of the Skeena and Nass Rivers.</p><p>The largest territory they historically occupied ran from the Portland Canal along the present border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaska">Alaska</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-columbia">British Columbia</a> north to the coast just south-east of the Copper River delta, and included almost all of the Alexander Archipelago. The Coastal Tlingit controlled one of the mountain passes into the Yukon interior. Inland Tlingit live along the Alsek, Tatshenshini, Chilkat, Taku and Stikine rivers and around Atlin, Teslin and Tagish Lakes, where they built trade networks with Athabascan peoples and often intermarried with them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Tlingit society is divided into two moieties, the Raven and the Eagle, which are divided into clans and then into lineages or house groups. Kinship is matrilineal, so that children are born into their mother's clan, and property and hereditary roles pass through the mother's line. Each group has heraldic crests, shown on totem poles, canoes, feast dishes, house posts and weavings. Rank rests on wealth and economic power but also on generosity and proper behaviour, and a rich tradition of oratory is valued.</p><p>Hereditary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> was practised extensively until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> outlawed it. The Tlingit built cedar plank houses, now called clan houses, and rely on a diet in which salmon receives the most emphasis, with seal and game close behind, along with halibut, herring, eulachon, shellfish and berries. Traditionally they were animists, and shamans cured disease and aided hunting; a central belief was the reincarnation of humans and animals.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Tlingit had their first contact with Europeans in 1741, with Russian explorers, and Spanish explorers followed in 1775. They kept their independence but suffered epidemics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> and other diseases brought by Europeans, and the smallpox epidemic of 1862 killed about 60 percent of the Mainland Tlingit and 37 percent of the Island Tlingit. Between 1886 and 1895, when shamans could not treat Old World diseases, many converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox Christianity</a>, whose missionaries had translated their liturgy into Tlingit.</p><p>Alaska Tlingit have no reservations, since the Alaska Native Claims Settlement Act set up regional corporations, among them Sealaska. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> the Tlingit belong to First Nations in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-columbia">British Columbia</a> and Yukon. The language has an estimated 200 to 400 native speakers in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and 100 in Canada, and several institutions run revival programmes.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tlingit” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Company's chief managers ruled from Kodiak and, from 1808, from New Archangel (Sitka), built by Alexander Baranov after the <b>Tlingit</b> destroyed an earlier fort in 1802.</blockquote><a href="/state/russian-america">Russian America</a></li></ul></section>
